---

<CaseSection id="context" standfirst="Two accounts payable teams in one property management company, paying very different bills.">
  <p>
    The client is a property management company with two AP teams. Family office AP pays invoices for properties held
    across many holding companies, so every invoice has to land on the right owner. Industrial AP pays the bills of a
    multi-tenant industrial portfolio, most of them utilities, and nothing is paid until the head of accounting approves
    the batch.
  </p>
  <dl class="users">
    <div><dt>AP specialists</dt><dd>Key almost every invoice, by hand, into the accounting system.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Head of accounting</dt><dd>Approves every payment batch and carries the risk of a missed bill.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Accounting lead</dt><dd>Runs the family office side and approves its invoices.</dd></div>
  </dl>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection
  id="problem"
  standfirst="Invoices arrive four ways, each one means typing ten fields, and nobody could say whether every bill had been paid."
>
  <p>
    Invoices arrived by email, through a Monday.com approval, from utility portals and in the post. Each meant typing
    ten fields into Yardi and looking up which holding company owned the property. Utility bills were downloaded one
    portal at a time and carried no invoice number. Every invoice description was typed by hand, and every new vendor
    was created twice, once in Yardi and again in Avid.
  </p>
  <Stats
    items={[
      { value: "4 ways", label: "invoices arrive: email, a Monday.com approval, utility portals and the post", source: "README:17" },
      { value: "10 fields", label: "typed by hand for every invoice, plus a lookup of the owning company", source: "README:17" },
      { value: "2 systems", label: "every new vendor is created in, one after the other", source: "README:18" },
    ]}
  />
  <p>
    The cost that mattered most was not time. Nobody could say whether every bill on a vacant unit had been paid, and a
    missed utility bill means a service shut off in a tenant's unit.
  </p>
  <Quote by="The head of accounting, industrial team" where="discovery session, July 2026" source="T1:56:13">
    For me the most priority item is making sure that we have paid everything or that services aren't getting shut off.
    ... Their water gets shut off and then we've got an unhappy tenant and that's an actual impact to our revenue stream.
  </Quote>
  <Quote by="The same speaker" where="discovery session, July 2026" source="T1:34:29">
    The real time suck is reading the invoice and properly putting in this long description and not making typos and
    doing it in a description naming scheme that we like.
  </Quote>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection
  id="research"
  standfirst="A kickoff with both teams, screen-share sessions on the real systems, written follow-ups, then a demo of all eight modules."
>
  <ul>
    <li>A kickoff with both teams in June 2026, then discovery sessions in July with each team sharing its screen.</li>
    <li>
      The industrial AP specialist walked through her invoice queue; the family office specialist keyed a test invoice
      in Yardi; a second industrial session went through the workbook the team uses to track utility bills.
    </li>
    <li>Written follow-ups and evidence sheets from both teams in August.</li>
    <li>
      I turned the evidence into demo specs, ruling on each open question, and on 9 September 2026 I walked both teams
      through all eight modules.
    </li>
  </ul>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection
  id="decisions"
  standfirst="A person stays in the loop, code does the arithmetic, every field says where it came from, and the screens show the work."
>
  <Decision title="A person stays in the loop">
    <p>
      The tools draft, flag and suggest. The specialist submits, the approver approves, and nothing is posted or paid
      automatically. AP Inbox adds no approval step of its own, because two already exist.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Code does the arithmetic; the model only reads">
    <p>
      Money maths, matching, vacancy logic and status rules are plain code. Model steps only read a document and return
      checked fields, each with a canned fallback, so the demo runs offline with no API key.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Honest confidence">
    <p>
      In AP Inbox every field shows where it came from: the document, the vendor's history, the parcel database, a
      default, or typing. In the reconciliation, payments matched by amount and date rather than account number are drawn
      lighter and say so.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="The meter is the key">
    <p>
      Account numbers change with every tenant; the meter does not. So the utility register is keyed on the meter, and
      every bill can be traced to the unit it serves.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Show the work, not vanity numbers">
    <p>
      No hours-saved counters, animated charts or chat boxes. The head of accounting ranked the shut-off risk above any
      time saving, and the utility demo runs two months so the room watches the second come out cleaner than the first.
    </p>
    <Quote by="The head of accounting, industrial team" where="discovery session, July 2026" source="T1:59:10">
      This for me, I mean, is worth way more than any time savings that any AI solution could get for downloading bills
      or anything because it really is affecting like revenue.
    </Quote>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="The team's own words">
    <p>
      Screens are named for the step as the team describes it ("Which bills are paid", "Needs a person"), and no screen
      says model, pipeline or API.
    </p>
  </Decision>
  <Decision title="Write access, payments and autopay" notBuilt>
    <p>
      The utility stack makes no payments, sets up no autopay and logs in to no live portal. The description writer is
      paste-ready and never writes into Avid. The vendor sync uses no model at all: a person decides every near match.
    </p>
  </Decision>
</CaseSection>

