  <h3>How it was built</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Specs first. Each module began as a demo pack: the data profile, business rules, screens, demo flow and numbered
      acceptance checks.
    </li>
    <li>
      A small team of agents. Three modules were built in parallel, each by a coordinator that fans out to four
      subagents: a seed builder, a logic builder, a UI builder and a rehearsal runner that writes one end-to-end check per
      acceptance check.
    </li>
    <li>
      Guardrails as code: deterministic seeds, identifier formats, an em dash check, and a leak guard for real names and
      figures whose lists live outside the repository.
    </li>
    <li>
      Hosted on Cloudflare Workers, with a separate demo state for every visitor.
      <a href="https://github.com/kakkiris/ap-automation-demo">The code is on GitHub</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="outcome" standfirst="Demonstrated to both teams in September 2026, reshaped by their feedback, and pitched as a phased build.">
  <p>
    On 9 September 2026 I walked both teams through all eight modules. Their feedback changed the plan: the standalone
    owner lookup folded into AP Inbox, and the description writer will read invoices already in Avid. On 18 September
    the team pitched a first phase: the meter register, utility bills to Yardi and paid-or-not, then AP Inbox once the
    client's parcel system is live.
  </p>
  <Quote by="The accounting lead, family office team" where="validation demo, September 2026" source="T4:01:06:17">
    I think we're on the right track again. Once we get to the weeds here, there could be a couple tweaks needed.
  </Quote>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="learned" standfirst="Let the model read and let code decide; build for the priority the client names.">
  <p>
    Let the model read and let code decide. Every number on screen has a rule behind it, so the demo runs offline and
    nobody has to trust a guess.
  </p>
  <p>
    Build for the priority the client names, not the easiest metric. The head of accounting ranked the shut-off risk
    above any time saving, so the screens show the work instead of hours saved.
  </p>
</CaseSection>

<CaseSection id="role" standfirst="Specs, product calls and the Claude Code build, plus the validation demo.">
  <div class="readme">
    <p>
      I worked from the discovery sessions with both AP teams, turned them into demo specs with numbered acceptance
      checks, made the product calls during the build, directed Claude Code through each build session, and led the
      validation demo with both teams.
    </p>
    <p class="note">From the repository README, word for word.</p>
  </div>
</CaseSection>
